  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1210 AM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed; the ridge that has been \nin place over the Four Corners region is still likely to stay \nnearly stationary over the next week. By Saturday, guidance \nshows a signal that the ridge will weaken as a trough moves \nalong the U.S./Canada border. Throughout the week, there should\nbe a few shortwaves moving through the area.\n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to \"cool\" down to the mid \nto high 90s. Winds look to remain under 25 MPH with RH values \nstaying above 25%, minimizing fire weather concerns.\n\nTuesday's PoPs are currently forecast to be in the 30-60% range, \nwith the highest chances in eastern Colorado. Guidance indicates \nthat the surface low to the south of the CWA will likely remain\nthere throughout the day. With the low stationary, winds should\nmainly be from the east-northeast, promoting upslope flow for \nshowers and storms. After looking at the instability and shear \nfor severe storm potential, there is a low end chance that they \nwould be severe, something we will need to watch over the next \nfew days. For the northwestern portions of the CWA, there is a \n25-40% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of precipitation from Tuesday \nafternoon into early Wednesday morning.\n\nWednesday still look to have higher precipitation chances of the \nlong-term period as a shortwave propagates through during the \nafternoon and evening hours. Guidance indicates a boundary moving in \nfrom the northwest toward the southeast, though models disagree on \nits exact location and timing. Currently, both the GFS and Canadian \nmodels show the boundary. However, the GFS brings it in during the \nlate afternoon hour, whereas the Canadian delays it until early\nThursday morning. Other guidance doesn't have the boundary. If \nit does materialize, the severe storm potential is again low, \nbut not 0. PoPs are forecast in the 40-65 range, giving eastern \nColorado the highest PoPs. Eastern Colorado also has a 25-45% \nchance of seeing 0.10\" of precipitation. Give this is roughly \nfour days out, it will be interesting to see how guidance trends\nfor the location and speed of the boundary.\n\nFor the rest of the week and weekend, PoPs steadily decrease. \nThursday's chances are around 20-30% while Friday-Sunday stay around \n10-20%. However, with shortwaves passing through, any favorable \nlifting mechanism could increase the precipitation chances.",
